          "text": "Good — that keeps stdio a pure transport + lifecycle change with zero policy work. Same attestation, new transport. Here's where it lands.\n\n## Settled\n\n| | decision |\n|---|---|\n| entry shape | `{ type: \"stdio\", command, args?, env?, cwd? }`, `type` explicit, `additionalProperties: false` |\n| interpolation | `{env:…}`/`{path:…}` in `command`, `args[]`, `env` values — resolved values feed `normalizeProtectedValues` |\n| bare `-e VAR` | rejected at boot, or at minimum documented — llame can't redact a secret it never resolved |\n| transport | own ~200-line `MCPTransport` impl: bounded 1 MiB read buffer, `stdin.end()`→SIGTERM→SIGKILL ladder, `detached` + group kill, `shell: false` |\n| protocol pin | read `transport.protocolVersion` post-connect, close if outside the three revisions (library also accepts `2024-11-05`) |\n| stderr | `'pipe'` → bounded ring buffer → protected-value sanitizer → diagnostic log with server id |\n| policy | unchanged. Operator attests read-only, same as HTTP |\n| tool ids, allowlist, drift, receipts, snapshots | untouched |\n\n## Three things still genuinely open\n\n**1. Eager spawn at boot — I think the architecture already forces it.** Lazy spawn is the obvious resource win, but the availability manifest is authored at Run-accept from the process-local catalog. A server with no catalog yet is `unavailable`, so the *first* Run against a lazily-spawned server would emit an availability reminder to the model and refuse the tool, then work on the second. That's worse than a browser idling. Recommend eager, matching HTTP exactly, and document the cost (`npx @playwright/mcp` = a chromium alive from boot). If that's unacceptable, the fix isn't lazy spawn, it's `enabled: false` / `startup: false` as an operator switch.\n\n**2. Crash-loop needs a state HTTP doesn't have.** Reconnect-forever at `min(5min, 2^n)` is right for a socket and wrong for a spawn: a typo'd `command`, a missing image, or a bad `npx` package respawns until the heat death of the deployment. The distinction worth encoding:\n\n```\nspawn failed (ENOENT) or exited <Ns after spawn, N times consecutively\n        → terminal \"misconfigured\", stop retrying until restart\nconnected, discovered, then died\n        → today's backoff, forever\n```\n\nThe first is a config error and should look like one. The second is an outage and llame's existing semantics are correct.\n\n**3. Concurrency semantics I didn't flag earlier.** One child per server per process, shared by every concurrent Run in that process. JSON-RPC ids multiplex fine, and it's no different from HTTP (one client, one session, shared). But for stateful stdio servers it bites harder: `workers.all.runs: 8` means eight Runs driving **one browser**, stepping on each other's tabs. Filesystem/search/sqlite servers don't care. Browser servers do. That's a docs-level warning, not a mechanism — unless you want per-Run server instances, which is a much bigger design and I'd argue against it.\n\nRefresh: re-`tools/list` on the live child, never recycle the process. Recycling turns a routine 48–72min refresh into a browser restart.\n\n## Small stuff worth carrying into the proposal\n\n- Push pinned versions in docs. `npx -y pkg@latest` re-fetches on every spawn in every process, changes the catalog between restarts (visible as drift churn), and fails closed on an air-gapped host with an error that won't point at the network.\n- Docker examples should carry `--init` (the Playwright docs use it) so the container reaps its own children; `--rm` so a crash-loop doesn't accumulate containers.\n- A hard-killed llame leaves orphans regardless of `detached`. `--rm --init` plus the SIGKILL ladder covers the normal paths; document that `docker ps` is the recovery tool for the abnormal one.\n- E2E gets *easier*: the existing fixture MCP server becomes a small node script over stdio instead of an HTTP server on a port, which removes `E2E_MCP_URL` port juggling.\n\n## Artifacts this touches\n\nFive documented refusals to reverse (`openspec/specs/mcp-tools/spec.md`'s reject-stdio requirement, `SPEC.md` §99, `VISION.md:156`, the schema's `type` enum description, `config-loader.test.ts:506`), plus `docs/mcp-tools.md` in three places, `apps********S.md`, README, CHANGELOG, and a `docs/scaling.md` note that split topology means every `web`-profile process also holds every child.\n\n---\n\nWant me to write this up? Two options, and they're not exclusive:\n\n- **`docs/research/tool-harness/2026-08-12-mcp-stdio.md`** — pinned-sources research doc in the same style as the transport one: peer config-surface survey, the `@ai-sdk/mcp` vs official-SDK transport delta with file:line, the env-inheritance finding, what llame must build. Provenance for the decisions above.\n- **An OpenSpec change proposal** — `proposal.md` + `design.md` + delta specs, with the three open items above written as decisions rather than questions.\n\nI'd do the research doc first — the design decisions lean on facts (env allowlist, unbounded buffer, missing kill ladder, API-side discovery) that deserve a citable home before a proposal asserts them.",
