GELOG.md`   | \"no stdio fallback\" phrasing                                              |\n\nPlus one addition: `docs/scaling.md` should record that in a split topology\nevery process — including `web`-profile API processes — holds a child process\nper configured stdio server (Finding 5).\n\n---\n\n## What I could not verify\n\n- **Nothing was spawned.** Every claim about runtime behavior is read from\n  source. In particular the Node `spawn({ signal })` → default `killSignal`\n  (`SIGTERM`) semantics, the orphaning of `npx`/`docker` descendant processes,\n  and the unauthenticated-start consequence of a bare `-e VAR` were reasoned\n  from documented semantics and the env allowlist, not reproduced.\n- Whether `waitForAbort(this.transport.close({ signal }), signal)` in\n  `mcp-client.ts:515` reliably completes the abort before the race resolves —\n  `StdioMCPTransport.close()` aborts synchronously on its first line\n  (`:101`), which suggests it does, but this was not exercised against a hung\n  child.\n- LibreChat's stdio process model: whether servers are spawned once per\n  instance or per user, and whether `env` / `initTimeout` / `timeout` apply to\n  stdio entries. The fetched docs page shows those fields only on HTTP\n  examples, and does not state the process model.\n- Whether a later `@ai-sdk/mcp` patch on the `1.0.x` / `ai-v6` line changes any\n  of Findings 1–4. Re-check the installed version before implementation if\n  meaningful time has passed.\n- Windows behavior throughout. Process groups, `detached`, and signal\n  semantics differ materially; llame targets Linux and this research did not\n  investigate the Windows path.\n- Whether llame's existing per-call timeout and abort plumbing behaves\n  identically over a stdio transport — assumed, since it lives above the\n  transport interface, but not tested.\n",
