            "new_string": "## Finding 4 — the official SDK's `StdioClientTransport` closes the two gaps that matter, and a llame-authored transport is not justified\n\n`@modelcontextprotocol/sdk@1.29.0`'s stdio client is the same lineage as the AI\nSDK's copy — identical env allowlist, identical unbounded `ReadBuffer` — but its\npublic surface (`dist/esm/client/stdio.d.ts`) carries two accessors the AI SDK\nversion does not, and a real teardown sequence.\n\n**Teardown** (`dist/esm/client/stdio.js:152-166`):\n\n```\nstdin.end()  →  race(close, 2s)  →  SIGTERM if exitCode === null\n             →  race(close, 2s)  →  SIGKILL if exitCode === null\n```\n\n**Readable stderr**, which is the fix for Finding 2's unfixable leak:\n\n```ts\n/** ... If stderr piping was requested, a PassThrough stream is returned\n    _immediately_, allowing callers to attach listeners before the start\n    method is invoked. This prevents loss of any early error output. */\nget stderr(): Stream | null;\nget pid(): number | null;\n```\n\nThe transport owns the PassThrough and pipes the child's stderr into it\ninternally (`stdio.js:97-98`), sidestepping the Node fd restriction entirely.\nllame passes `stderr: 'pipe'`, attaches to `transport.stderr` before `start()`,\nbounds it to a ring buffer, and runs it through the existing protected-value\nsanitizer. That is precisely the policy llame needs, with no custom code.\n\n| gap                | `@ai-sdk/mcp@1.0.67`         | official `StdioClientTransport`                    | llame-authored |\n| ------------------ | ---------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------- | -------------- |\n| teardown ladder    | SIGTERM only, no escalation  | **yes**                                            | yes            |\n| readable stderr    | **no** (Finding 2)           | **yes** (`get stderr`)                             | yes            |\n| bounded readbuffer | no                           | no — same unbounded `Buffer.concat` (`shared/stdio.js:5-24`) | yes  |\n| process-group kill | no                           | no — `pid` is exposed, but the child is spawned without `detached`, so it is not a group leader and `kill(-pid)` does not apply | yes |\n| protocol pin       | post-connect, either way     | same                                               | same           |\n| cost               | zero                         | zero + a direct dependency                         | ~200 lines, permanently diverging |\n\n**Conclusion: adopt the official `StdioClientTransport`; do not author one.**\nThe two residual gaps do not justify a permanent fork of upstream code:\n\n- The unbounded read buffer is a robustness concern against an\n  operator-installed local process, not a remote attacker. It should be\n  recorded as a known limitation, not solved by reimplementation.\n- The tree-kill gap is narrower than it first appears (Finding 2's correction:\n  docker forwards signals; a direct binary is one process; only `npx` is\n  shaky), and the crash-loop terminal state in Finding 6 bounds the\n  accumulation.\n\nThe cost is promoting `@modelcontextprotocol/sdk` from a transitive `shadcn`\ndependency to a direct runtime dependency of `apps/api`, and running two MCP\nimplementations in one process. The AI SDK explicitly designs for this — its own\ndocs recommend the combination.\n\nThe interfaces are structurally compatible: `setProtocolVersion?` is optional on\nboth (`shared/transport.d.ts:87`, `mcp-transport.ts:86`), and the official\n`close(): Promise<void>` satisfies `close(options?)`. Two integration details to\nconfirm with `tsgo` rather than assume: `send`'s options parameter is\n`TransportSendOptions` on one side and `MCPTransportSendOptions` on the other,\nand the official `Transport` does not declare `protocolVersion`, so the AI SDK\nclient assigns it dynamically (`mcp-client.ts:497-500`) and reading it back for\nthe protocol gate needs a narrow accessor type rather than a cast (`as unknown\nas T` is banned repo-wide, #268).\n\nConfidence: high for the transport surfaces and the recommendation; the TS\ncompatibility of `send` is **unverified** — see \"what I could not verify.\"",
            "old_string": "## Finding 4 — the official SDK has the teardown ladder the AI SDK copy dropped, and neither bounds its read buffer\n\n`@modelcontextprotocol/sdk@1.29.0`'s `StdioClientTransport.close()`\n(`dist/esm/client/stdio.js:152-166`) does what a supervisor should:\n\n```\nstdin.end()  →  race(close, 2s)  →  SIGTERM if exitCode === null\n             →  race(close, 2s)  →  SIGKILL if exitCode === null\n```\n\n`@ai-sdk/mcp`'s copy has none of it. If llame builds its own transport\n(Finding 1), this ladder is the thing to port — it is 15 lines and it is the\ndifference between a wedged MCP server being cleaned up and being leaked.\n\nNeither implementation bounds its read buffer: the official `ReadBuffer`\n(`dist/esm/shared/stdio.js:5-24`) is the same unbounded `Buffer.concat`. So\nswitching to the official SDK would fix teardown and not the memory bound, at\nthe cost of adding a second MCP SDK to the dependency graph for a transport\nllame would still have to wrap. **Implementing `MCPTransport` directly (~200\nlines against `node:child_process`) is the better trade** and also sheds the\n`Experimental_` prefix on a pinned `1.0.x` line.\n\nConfidence: high.",
