            "command": "cat > /tmp/claude-1000/-home-leon0399--herdr-worktrees-llame-feature-mcp-stdio/574c75d3-0ab6-4514-ace5-7741df66ed6d/scratchpad/issue.md << 'EOF'\n## Problem\n\nllame's tool catalog is bounded by fixed v1 limits — 256 tools per page, 1,000 tools total, 4 MiB retained declarations (`mcp-tools` spec, \"Discovery is complete, bounded, and isolated\"). Every admitted tool's declaration is then sent to the model on every turn.\n\nTwo limits are conflated in that design:\n\n- **Wire-level resource guards** — per-message and aggregate byte bounds, the discovery deadline, the page cap, the cursor guard. These protect the process from a hostile or broken server and are correct as fixed constants.\n- **Context-window budget** — how many tool declarations can be put in front of the model before they crowd out the conversation. This is a *per-model* quantity, and llame currently has no notion of it at all.\n\nToday the two are indistinguishable because the catalog is small. `search_conversations` plus a handful of operator-configured MCP tools is nowhere near 1,000 tools. Once operators wire up real MCP servers, that stops being true: `github-mcp-server --toolsets all` is ~100 tools, `@playwright/mcp` is ~60. Two or three such servers is a few hundred declarations shipped in full on every turn, against a `contextWindowTokens` that may be 128k on a local model.\n\nThe failure is quiet: no limit is breached, nothing errors, and compaction simply triggers sooner because a large fixed prefix eats the window. On a small-context model it can crowd out the conversation entirely.\n\n## What to explore\n\nA **context-window-proportional threshold** that defers tool declarations rather than sending them all. The reference implementation is Claude Code's, which is worth copying in shape:\n\n```\nthreshold = contextWindow(model) * percentage      // default 10%\nenabled   = deferredToolTokens >= threshold\n```\n\nTools above the threshold are not sent; the model is given their names plus a search tool and loads the schemas it needs on demand. The threshold is proportional rather than a binary large-model exemption — a 1M-context model gets a ~100k-token budget, not an unlimited one, and still trips the threshold with a big enough catalog.\n\nDesign questions this raises for llame specifically:\n\n- **Where does the threshold live?** Per-model config next to `contextWindowTokens` and `compactionThresholdTokens`, or a single instance-level percentage? Per-model matches the existing compaction precedent.\n- **How does deferral interact with the immutable Run snapshot?** A Run binds an exact set of tool ids and declaration hashes at accept time. If the model loads a declaration mid-run, is it bound at accept (all candidates hashed, subset sent) or at load time (which would mutate the bound set)? Binding everything at accept and varying only what is *sent* is the safer shape and preserves the drift contract.\n- **Does the availability manifest change?** A deferred tool is available but not advertised — a third state alongside available and unavailable, and it is model-facing, so it touches persisted parts.\n- **Does this replace the 1,000-tool cap?** Probably not: the cap is a resource guard and survives independently. Worth stating explicitly so the two limits stop being conflated.\n- **Does the tool-search tool itself need a permission model?** It enumerates tool names, which are already visible in the receipt, so likely not — but the allowlist filter must still apply to what it can surface.\n\n## Not in scope\n\nThe wire-level byte bounds and the discovery deadline. Those solve memory safety while reading a catalog, not context budget while sending one — deferring tools does not mean reading fewer bytes. They stay fixed.\n\n## Provenance\n\n`docs/research/tool-harness/2026-08-12-mcp-stdio.md` Finding 7 and the surrounding discussion, where the threshold mechanism was verified against the reference implementation while scoping stdio MCP support. Raised as a follow-up there because stdio makes large catalogs materially easier for an operator to acquire.\nEOF\ngh issue create --title \"design(api): context-window-proportional tool-search threshold instead of a flat catalog cap\" --label mcp --label future --body-file /tmp/claude-1000/-home-leon0399--herdr-worktrees-llame-feature-mcp-stdio/574c75d3-0ab6-4514-ace5-7741df66ed6d/scratchpad/issue.md",
